{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Jörg Leichtfried (born 18 June 1967 in Bruck an der Mur, Styria) is an Austrian politician who currently serves as Federal Minister for Transport, Innovation and Technology in the government of Chancellor Christian Kern.","title":"Jörg Leichtfried"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The chancellor of the College of William & Mary is the ceremonial head of the college, chosen by the university's Board of Visitors.","title":"Chancellor of the College of William &amp; Mary"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The office was created by the college's Royal Charter, which stipulated that the chancellor would serve a seven-year term.","title":"Chancellor of the College of William &amp; Mary"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Henry Compton, Bishop of London, was named in the Charter as the college's first chancellor.","title":"Chancellor of the College of William &amp; Mary"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Associate Justice of the Supreme Court of the United States Sandra Day O'Connor served as chancellor from 2005 until 2012 when Robert Gates assumed the office.","title":"Chancellor of the College of William &amp; Mary"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" He was installed as chancellor on February 3, 2012.","title":"Chancellor of the College of William &amp; Mary"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Kreisky–Peter–Wiesenthal affair was a political and personal feud in the 1970s fought between the then Austrian chancellor Bruno Kreisky and the Nazi hunter Simon Wiesenthal arising from Kreisky's ministerial appointments and the SS past of Freedom Party leader Friedrich Peter, which had been revealed by Wiesenthal.","title":"Kreisky–Peter–Wiesenthal affair"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Wolfgang Schüssel (] ; born 7 June 1945) is an Austrian People's Party politician.","title":"Wolfgang Schüssel"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was Chancellor of Austria for two consecutive terms from February 2000 to January 2007.","title":"Wolfgang Schüssel"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" While being recognised as a rare example of an active reformer in contemporary Austrian politics, his governments were also highly controversial from the beginning, starting with the fact that he formed a coalition government with Jörg Haider's Freedom Party of Austria (FPÖ) on both occasions.","title":"Wolfgang Schüssel"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" In 2011, he retired from being an active member of parliament due to a multitude of charges of corruption against members of his governments.","title":"Wolfgang Schüssel"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Otto Planetta (2 August 1899 in Vyškov, Austro-Hungarian Empire, now Czech Republic – 31 July 1934 in Vienna, Austria) was an Austrian Nazi.","title":"Otto Planetta"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" On 25 July 1934 he murdered Engelbert Dollfuss, Austrian Chancellor during the unsuccessful July Putsch, four years before the Anschluss.","title":"Otto Planetta"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He and the other assassins were members of SS Regiment 89.","title":"Otto Planetta"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He was executed.","title":"Otto Planetta"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"George Abraham von Dyhrn, "1st Baron of Dyhrn" (1620–1671), was an Austrian Chancellor in the province of Silesia, politician and a landowner in the Habsburg monarchy.","title":"George Abraham von Dyhrn"}],"peer_messages":[" London, was named in the Charter as the college's first chancellor.","title":"Chancellor of the College of William &amp; Mary"}
